# E2E coverage for user workflows

User-facing editor behavior is **system critical**. Unit tests alone are not enough.

## When adding or changing user-facing behavior

1. **Add or update a Playwright test** in `editor/tests/e2e/` that exercises the workflow end-to-end (browser + real server).
2. Prefer **mouse clicks and keyboard shortcuts** the user actually uses — not internal helpers or test-only hooks.
3. Use **`data-testid`** on new interactive UI (panels, forms, status) so tests stay stable.

## Core workflows that must stay covered

| Workflow | Spec (minimum) |
|----------|----------------|
| Load workspace file | `editor.spec.js` |
| Switch file | `workflows.spec.js` |
| Create new file | `workflows.spec.js` |
| Import (OPML / `.mm` / `.xmind`) | `workflows.spec.js` |
| Save + draft autosave | add when touched |
| Export PNG / SVG / markmap | add when touched |
| Edit map (nodes, markers, hotkeys) | `editor.spec.js`, `marker-layout.spec.js` |

If you add a new toolbar action, panel button, or context-menu item, extend the matching spec in the same PR.

## Do not

- Ship file/import/save/export UI without an e2e test.
- Use `prompt()`, `confirm()`, or `alert()` for core flows — use inline UI testable by Playwright (see `minimal-ui.mdc`).
- Disable or skip e2e in CI for convenience; e2e runs on **every PR and on main**.

## Verify locally

```bash
cd editor && npm ci && npx playwright install chromium && npm run test:e2e
```

Run `npm run test:all` before opening a PR that touches editor behavior.
